# BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding
# 9,219 spoken words, 765 nodes
SAY   BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding.
PAUSE 1200
SAY   Jacob Devlin Ming-Wei Chang Kenton Lee Kristina Toutanova Google
SPELL AI
SAY   Language
PAUSE 500
SAY   Abstract
PAUSE 500
SAY   We introduce a new language representation model called BERT, which stands for Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers. Unlike recent language representation models (Peters and others, 2018a; Radford and others, 2018), BERT is designed to pre-train deep bidirectional representations from unlabeled text by jointly conditioning on both left and right context in all layers. As a result, the pre-trained BERT model can be fine-tuned with just one additional output layer to create state-of-the-art models for a wide range of tasks, such as question answering and language inference, without substantial task-specific architecture modifications.
PAUSE 500
SAY   BERT is conceptually simple and empirically powerful. It obtains new state-of-the-art results on eleven natural language processing tasks, including pushing the GLUE score to 80.5 percent (7.7 percent point absolute improvement), MultiNLI accuracy to 86.7 percent (4.6 percent absolute improvement), SQuAD v1.1 question answering Test F1 to 93.2 (1.5 point absolute improvement) and SQuAD v2.0 Test F1 to 83.1 (5.1 point absolute improvement).
PAUSE 500
SAY   1 Introduction
PAUSE 500
SAY   Language model pre-training has been shown to be effective for improving many natural language processing tasks (Dai and Le, 2015; Peters and others, 2018a; Radford and others, 2018; Howard and Ruder, 2018). These include sentence-level tasks such as natural language inference (Bowman and others, 2015; Williams and others, 2018) and paraphrasing (Dolan and Brockett, 2005), which aim to predict the relationships between sentences by analyzing them holistically, as well as token-level tasks such as named entity recognition and question answering, where models are required to produce fine-grained output at the token level (Tjong Kim Sang and De Meulder, 2003; Rajpurkar and others, 2016).
PAUSE 500
SAY   There are two existing strategies for applying pre-trained language representations to down-stream tasks: feature-based and fine-tuning. The feature-based approach, such as ELMo (Peters and others, 2018a), uses task-specific architectures that include the pre-trained representations as additional features. The fine-tuning approach, such as the Generative Pre-trained Transformer (OpenAI GPT) (Radford and others, 2018), introduces minimal task-specific parameters, and is trained on the downstream tasks by simply fine-tuning all pre-trained parameters. The two approaches share the same objective function during pre-training, where they use unidirectional language models to learn general language representations.
PAUSE 500
SAY   We argue that current techniques restrict the power of the pre-trained representations, especially for the fine-tuning approaches. The major limitation is that standard language models are unidirectional, and this limits the choice of architectures that can be used during pre-training. For example, in OpenAI GPT, the authors use a left-to-right architecture, where every token can only attend to previous tokens in the self-attention layers of the Transformer (Vaswani and others, 2017). Such restrictions are sub-optimal for sentence-level tasks, and could be very harmful when applying fine-tuning based approaches to token-level tasks such as question answering, where it is crucial to incorporate context from both directions.
PAUSE 500
SAY   In this paper, we improve the fine-tuning based approaches by proposing BERT: Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers. BERT alleviates the previously mentioned unidirectionality constraint by using a "masked language model" (MLM) pre-training objective, inspired by the Cloze task (Taylor, 1953). The masked language model randomly masks some of the tokens from the input, and the objective is to predict the original vocabulary id of the masked
PAUSE 500
SAY   word based only on its context. Unlike left-to-right language model pre-training, the MLM objective enables the representation to fuse the left and the right context, which allows us to pre-train a deep bidirectional Transformer. In addition to the masked language model, we also use a "next sentence prediction" task that jointly pretrains text-pair representations. The contributions of our paper are as follows:
PAUSE 500
SAY   We demonstrate the importance of bidirectional pre-training for language representations. Unlike Radford and others (2018), which uses unidirectional language models for pre-training, BERT uses masked language models to enable pre-trained deep bidirectional representations. This is also in contrast to Peters and others (2018a), which uses a shallow concatenation of independently trained left-to-right and right-to-left LMs.
PAUSE 500
SAY   We show that pre-trained representations reduce the need for many heavily-engineered task-specific architectures. BERT is the first fine-tuning based representation model that achieves state-of-the-art performance on a large suite of sentence-level and token-level tasks, outperforming many task-specific architectures.
PAUSE 500
SAY   BERT advances the state of the art for eleven NLP tasks. The code and pre-trained models are available at link google-research/bert.
PAUSE 500
SAY   2 Related Work
PAUSE 500
SAY   There is a long history of pre-training general language representations, and we briefly review the most widely-used approaches in this section.
PAUSE 500
SAY   2.1 Unsupervised Feature-based Approaches
PAUSE 500
SAY   Learning widely applicable representations of words has been an active area of research for decades, including non-neural (Brown and others, 1992; Ando and Zhang, 2005; Blitzer and others, 2006) and neural (Mikolov and others, 2013; Pennington and others, 2014) methods. Pre-trained word embeddings are an integral part of modern NLP systems, offering significant improvements over embeddings learned from scratch (Turian and others, 2010). To pre-train word embedding vectors, left-to-right language modeling objectives have been used (Mnih and Hinton, 2009), as well as objectives to discriminate correct from incorrect words in left and right context (Mikolov and others, 2013).
PAUSE 500
SAY   These approaches have been generalized to coarser granularities, such as sentence embeddings (Kiros and others, 2015; Logeswaran and Lee, 2018) or paragraph embeddings (Le and Mikolov, 2014). To train sentence representations, prior work has used objectives to rank candidate next sentences (Jernite and others, 2017; Logeswaran and Lee, 2018), left-to-right generation of next sentence words given a representation of the previous sentence (Kiros and others, 2015), or denoising auto-encoder derived objectives (Hill and others, 2016).
PAUSE 500
SAY   ELMo and its predecessor (Peters and others, 2017, 2018a) generalize traditional word embedding research along a different dimension. They extract context-sensitive features from a left-to-right and a right-to-left language model. The contextual representation of each token is the concatenation of the left-to-right and right-to-left representations. When integrating contextual word embeddings with existing task-specific architectures, ELMo advances the state of the art for several major NLP benchmarks (Peters and others, 2018a) including question answering (Rajpurkar and others, 2016), sentiment analysis (Socher and others, 2013), and named entity recognition (Tjong Kim Sang and De Meulder, 2003). Melamud and others (2016) proposed learning contextual representations through a task to predict a single word from both left and right context using LSTMs. Similar to ELMo, their model is feature-based and not deeply bidirectional. Fedus and others (2018) shows that the cloze task can be used to improve the robustness of text generation models.
PAUSE 500
SAY   2.2 Unsupervised Fine-tuning Approaches
PAUSE 500
SAY   As with the feature-based approaches, the first works in this direction only pre-trained word embedding parameters from unlabeled text (Collobert and Weston, 2008).
PAUSE 500
SAY   More recently, sentence or document encoders which produce contextual token representations have been pre-trained from unlabeled text and fine-tuned for a supervised downstream task (Dai and Le, 2015; Howard and Ruder, 2018; Radford and others, 2018). The advantage of these approaches is that few parameters need to be learned from scratch. At least partly due to this advantage, OpenAI GPT (Radford and others, 2018) achieved previously state-of-the-art results on many sentence-level tasks from the GLUE benchmark (Wang and others, 2018a). Left-to-right language model-
