SAY   A.1 Illustration of the Pre-training Tasks
PAUSE 500
SAY   We provide examples of the pre-training tasks in the following.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Masked LM and the Masking Procedure Assuming the unlabeled sentence is my dog is
PAUSE 500
SAY   hairy, and during the random masking procedure we chose the 4-th token (which corresponding to
PAUSE 500
SAY   hairy), our masking procedure can be further illustrated by
PAUSE 500
SAY   80 percent of the time: Replace the word with the [MASK] token, for example, my dog is hairy →
PAUSE 500
SAY   my dog is [MASK]
PAUSE 500
SAY   10 percent of the time: Replace the word with a random word, for example, my dog is hairy →my
PAUSE 500
SAY   dog is apple
PAUSE 500
SAY   10 percent of the time: Keep the word unchanged, for example, my dog is hairy →my dog
PAUSE 500
SAY   is hairy. The purpose of this is to bias the representation towards the actual observed word.
PAUSE 500
SAY   The advantage of this procedure is that the Transformer encoder does not know which words it will be asked to predict or which have been replaced by random words, so it is forced to keep a distributional contextual representation of every input token. Additionally, because random replacement only occurs for 1.5 percent of all tokens (that is, 10 percent of 15 percent), this does not seem to harm the model's language understanding capability. In Section C.2, we evaluate the impact this procedure.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Compared to standard langauge model training, the masked LM only make predictions on 15 percent of tokens in each batch, which suggests that more pre-training steps may be required for the model
